*(
    title: Polar Form and de Moivre's Theorem
    description: Writing a complex number by its modulus and argument, so that multiplying multiplies lengths and adds angles.
    tags: complex numbers, polar form, modulus argument form, de moivre, euler, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Cartesian form is built for addition. Adding $a + ib$ to $c + id$ means adding $a$ to $c$ and $b$ to $d$, and nothing could be more direct.

    Multiplication gets no such help. Expanding $(a + ib)(c + id)$ produces $ac - bd$ and $ad + bc$, two expressions that mix all four inputs and say nothing about what the product *is*. The operation has a simple description; the coordinates are the wrong coordinates to see it in.

    ===

    The modulus and the argument are the right ones. Both are already defined for any $z$: the distance from the origin, and the angle from the positive real axis. Reading the canvas by those two instead of by $\operatorname{Re}(z)$ and $\operatorname{Im}(z)$ gives $r = 2$ and $\theta = \frac{\pi}{3}$ for the point drawn there.

    definition{
        A representation of a complex number as $z = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)$, abbreviated $r\operatorname{cis}\theta$, in which $r = |z|$ is its modulus and $\theta = \arg z$ is its argument.
    }(
        term: Polar form
    )

    Converting is trigonometry on the right-angled triangle. Going out, $r = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$ and $\tan\theta = \frac{b}{a}$ with the quadrant taken from the sketch; coming back, $a = r\cos\theta$ and $b = r\sin\theta$. Here $2\cos\frac{\pi}{3} = 1$ and $2\sin\frac{\pi}{3} = \sqrt{3}$, which returns the Cartesian form it started from.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        segment(start: (0, 0); end: (1, 0); colour: grey; dashed)
        segment(start: (1, 0); end: (1, 1.7321); colour: grey; dashed)
        point(x: 1; y: 1.7321; colour: orange; label: $z = 1 + i\sqrt{3}$)

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (0, 0); end: (1, 1.7321); colour: orange)
            text(label: $r = 2$; x: 0.35; y: 1.05)
            text(label: $\theta = \tfrac{\pi}{3}$; x: 0.62; y: 0.22)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
    )
]

scene{
    # Multiplying multiplies moduli and adds arguments

    Take $z_1 = 2\operatorname{cis}\frac{\pi}{3}$ and $z_2 = 3\operatorname{cis}\frac{\pi}{6}$, the two rays on the canvas. In Cartesian form they are $1 + i\sqrt{3}$ and $\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2} + \frac{3}{2}i$, and multiplying them out is four terms of bookkeeping.

    ===

    The answer is $6i$ — modulus $6$, argument $\frac{\pi}{2}$. The moduli $2$ and $3$ have multiplied; the arguments $\frac{\pi}{3}$ and $\frac{\pi}{6}$ have added.

    ===

    That is not a coincidence of these numbers. Expanding $r_1\operatorname{cis}\theta_1 \cdot r_2\operatorname{cis}\theta_2$ and collecting terms produces $\cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2 - \sin\theta_1\sin\theta_2$ for the real part and $\sin\theta_1\cos\theta_2 + \cos\theta_1\sin\theta_2$ for the imaginary part — the compound-angle expansions of $\cos(\theta_1 + \theta_2)$ and $\sin(\theta_1 + \theta_2)$.

    equation{
        r_1\operatorname{cis}\theta_1 \cdot r_2\operatorname{cis}\theta_2 = r_1 r_2 \operatorname{cis}(\theta_1 + \theta_2)
    }

    So multiplying by a complex number is a scaling and a rotation performed together: stretch by $r_2$, turn by $\theta_2$. Division reverses both, dividing the moduli and subtracting the arguments. Multiplying by $i$ — modulus $1$, argument $\frac{\pi}{2}$ — leaves every length alone and turns the plane a quarter turn anticlockwise.

    note{
        Adding arguments can push the total outside $-\pi < \theta \le \pi$, so a product written in principal form may need $2\pi$ added or subtracted. The direction is unchanged; only the name of the angle is.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        segment(start: (0, 0); end: (1, 1.7321); colour: orange)
        point(x: 1; y: 1.7321; colour: orange; label: $z_1$)
        segment(start: (0, 0); end: (2.5981, 1.5); colour: purple)
        point(x: 2.5981; y: 1.5; colour: purple; label: $z_2$)

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (0, 0); end: (0, 6); colour: teal)

            cue.highlight{
                point(x: 0; y: 6; colour: teal; label: $z_1 z_2 = 6i$)
            }(
                at: 2
                duration: 2s
            )
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
    )
]

scene{
    # de Moivre's theorem

    A power is repeated multiplication, so the product rule applies to itself. Squaring squares the modulus and doubles the argument; cubing cubes the modulus and trebles the argument.

    definition{
        A theorem stating that $(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta)^n = \cos n\theta + i\sin n\theta$ for every integer $n$, so that in polar form $(r\operatorname{cis}\theta)^n = r^n \operatorname{cis}(n\theta)$.
    }(
        term: de Moivre's theorem
    )

    For positive $n$ this follows from the product rule by induction: it holds at $n = 1$, and multiplying $\operatorname{cis}(k\theta)$ by $\operatorname{cis}\theta$ adds one more $\theta$ to give $\operatorname{cis}((k + 1)\theta)$. Negative exponents follow from the division rule, and $n = 0$ is $\operatorname{cis}0 = 1$.

    ===

    The canvas takes $z = \operatorname{cis}\frac{\pi}{6}$, whose modulus is $1$. Raising it to a power leaves the modulus at $1^n = 1$, so every power stays on the unit circle and only the angle moves — a step of $30^\circ$ each time.

    ===

    Six steps of $30^\circ$ reach $180^\circ$, so $z^6 = \operatorname{cis}\pi = -1$. Reading that backwards is the more useful direction: $-1$ has a sixth root sitting at $30^\circ$, and the same walk run at other starting angles will find the others.

    The theorem is what makes powers of complex numbers tractable at all. Expanding $(1 + i\sqrt{3})^{10}$ term by term is a binomial expansion of eleven terms; in polar form it is $2^{10}\operatorname{cis}\frac{10\pi}{3}$, or $1024\operatorname{cis}\left(-\tfrac{2\pi}{3}\right)$ once the angle is brought back into principal range.

    There is a second way to raise $\operatorname{cis}\theta$ to the power $n$, which is to expand the bracket binomially. Both are correct, so their real parts agree and so do their imaginary parts — and each agreement is a trigonometric identity. *de Moivre and Trigonometric Identities* collects them.

    internote(ref: de-moivre-and-trigonometric-identities.chalk)
}[
    canvas.graph{
        circle(x: 0; y: 0; size: 1; colour: grey; !fill)
        point(x: 0.866; y: 0.5; colour: orange; label: $z$)

        cue{
            point(x: 0.5; y: 0.866; colour: teal)
            point(x: 0; y: 1; colour: teal; label: $z^3$)
        }(
            in: 1
        )

        cue{
            point(x: -0.5; y: 0.866; colour: teal)
            point(x: -0.866; y: 0.5; colour: teal)
            point(x: -1; y: 0; colour: teal; label: $z^6 = -1$)
        }(
            in: 2
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        !show ticks
    )
]

scene{
    ## Euler's form

    The rules of polar form are the rules of indices wearing a disguise. Multiplying multiplies $r$ and adds $\theta$; a power raises $r$ and multiplies $\theta$. Anything behaving that way under multiplication is an exponential in $\theta$.

    definition{
        An identity stating that $e^{i\theta} = \cos\theta + i\sin\theta$ for every real $\theta$, so that any complex number can be written $z = re^{i\theta}$ with $r = |z|$ and $\theta = \arg z$.
    }(
        term: Euler's formula
    )

    ===

    Written this way the product rule needs no proof and no compound-angle identities: $r_1e^{i\theta_1} \cdot r_2e^{i\theta_2} = r_1r_2e^{i(\theta_1 + \theta_2)}$ is the index law $e^{x}e^{y} = e^{x+y}$. de Moivre's theorem is $\left(e^{i\theta}\right)^n = e^{in\theta}$, which is the other index law.

    ===

    Putting $\theta = \pi$ gives $e^{i\pi} = \cos\pi + i\sin\pi = -1$, the identity usually written $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$. It is not a separate curiosity: it is the statement that turning through half a revolution sends $1$ to $-1$.

    Read that way, an equation like $z^n = 1$ stops being algebra and becomes a question about angles: which rotations, repeated $n$ times, bring a number back to where it started? Matching moduli forces $r = 1$, and matching arguments forces $n\theta$ to be a whole number of revolutions — so the solutions can be counted and drawn rather than solved for.

    There turn out to be exactly $n$ of them, sitting at the corners of a regular polygon on the unit circle. *Roots of Unity* works that out and uses it to factorise $z^n - 1$ completely.

    internote(ref: roots-of-unity.chalk)
}[
    #### The three forms of one number

    equation{
        z = a + ib = r(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta) = re^{i\theta}
    }

    cue{
        #### Products are index laws

        equation{
            r_1e^{i\theta_1} \cdot r_2e^{i\theta_2} = r_1 r_2 e^{i(\theta_1 + \theta_2)}
        }

        equation{
            \left(re^{i\theta}\right)^{n} = r^{n}e^{in\theta}
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### Half a turn

        equation{
            e^{i\pi} = \cos\pi + i\sin\pi = -1
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )
]
